ut  i think when it comes to um whether the  trainings can be delivered effectively  online or not i think there is no  question to be asked there we have like  there is  data points of millions of people who  have learned effectively  it's it's a individual personal choice i  don't think it's it's it's it's whether  one model works or another model works  okay  i agree with that  personal learning styles adult learning  theories  um  come into play here and sometimes that  guides or  guides our recommendations to certain  customers  but what the pandemic has shown through  kind of forcing people to operate in a  remote way is that  more people maybe can learn remotely  you know using the tools that are now  available then probably thought so  beforehand and do it successfully  an interesting question uh  because i don't know that we would  necessarily  agree with the  premise which is  uh  is  what the steps and best practices to  learn digitally without a teacher  and how is it different from manual or  in learning and i know krishna that  digital learning doesn't necessarily  mean that you don't have an instructor  or teacher with you that  in fact  you can have that as part of digital  learning  yeah so that's the boot camp way of  learning in a boot camp is pretty much  a a cohort based learning where an  instructor is is teaching a set of  students and taking through the entire  learning journey and and there are other  models of digital learning wherein there  are videos and and you self learn right  so obviously there are different models  but now this instructor-led bootcamp  model has become quite  quite an established category it used to  happen before the pandemic also but in  the pandemic since colleges and  universities schools everything moved  online there's much more acceptance of  this model we so so we have adapted to  this new way and there's a lot of  appreciation for this new model  right  we have a question from ramachandra who  uh  asks  will respilling and upskilling help  someone who needs to return to the  workforce  definitely i think there's no question  asked right  and plus the way industry is changing i  don't think there is any other available  to continuously keep uh be on the top of  your roll right because all roles are  going through a transformation lot of  new developments are happening a lot of  new skills are getting added to the  existing roles right so unless you are  on top of the game  there's no top of the game through  constant reskinning and upskilling there  is no way to  to strive and and thrive in your role  i would say it's continuous as well so  the most important thing is to develop a  culture of learning and personal  self-development which means that you  are always learning always trying to  pick up something new but  i think it's a very it right now is a  great time to be returning to the  workplace uh picking up some new skills  and identifying or even entering the  workplace and going well i can see the  skills that are going to be in demand  and i can align them with the things  that i'm passionate about  and uh you know the next five to ten  years looks very very interesting  and if you can get that venn diagram  where the thing you love overlaps with  the skills people need um you know  you've got you've got uh you've got a  real winner there  that leads into a question  uh  tahira who asks  uh  uh uh what are the what's the importance  of an employee  themselves taking the responsibility to  for  their upskilling their upgrading and how  do they make that a systematic part of  their long-term development plan  so nobody  uh well very few people get the luxury  anymore of working for one organization  for all of their career and you might  even say  um you know that's not even the most  recommended approach but it's it's very  rare indeed so  you know  your personal learning and development  journey is something that everybody must  take responsibility for i feel very  passionately about that myself  in terms of structurally working through  it you know working with somebody else  being able to just talk out loud to  somebody about your dreams and your  ambitions is a good way of codifying  your plans and thinking and if that  person is a mentor or somebody with a  bit more experience than you all the  better um  but but definitely if you're relying on  your employer to develop your career  you're making a mistake in my opinion i  mean they may help fund it they may  point you in the right direction they  may encourage you along that journey and  i hope they do good employers will but  if you have a at least a 50 or 51 pop to  play in that if not more  uh chris you have a last question um  there's a  question from kyle who asks about  how organizations like simply learn are  using partnerships with colleges and  with higher education  and how do you  select those partnerships  and what  makes those partnerships different  from other modes of  learning or training  so um  kyle from simply run perspective see we  have we have partnered with some of the  top institutions in the world and the  reason for our partnership is that those  institutions have got built over over a  really long period of time  and they have a history of  introducing topics and history of  teaching new concepts  in a way that not everybody understands  that pedagogy so when we are partnered  with an mit and caltech they are known  for teaching some of the toughest  technology related topics and their  experts some of their professors are  authority in themselves in themselves  when it comes to those topics right so  so our idea was to like build something  that's not otherwise easily available  for people from any part of the world to  go and learn from them so we wanted to  get the best of the world to partner  with us so that we can impart their  knowledge to a wider audience all across  the world  and our our our partnership philosophy  is like very very  crisp and clear we are only partnering  with the top 10 institutions in the  world right so we're not it's not a  partnership that you're looking at doing  it all kinds of colleges and  and  higher education institution is like  very very selective and very very um  based on based on a very detailed  criteria that we do and and  there are other reasons also if i'm  partnering with mit caltech and party on  one side i can't go and partner with  institutions who are pretty below lower  in the ranking some of these  institutions will not appreciate and no  will not like their name to be taken in  the same fashion as as many other  institutions so so that's that's that's  also is there is a driver when it comes  to selecting all partnerships  okay  we have one last question i think uh  maybe russ could take this  talk about  reskilling in apps like helping someone  return to the workforce follow-up from  that user is  are there special challenges for people  who are 50 or older  all their special challenges  for people who are 50 or older  because that was a special challenge for  someone who's looking to get back into  the workforce and looking to use  um maybe skilling programs as a path in  and i think that it shouldn't be um i'm  not saying it wouldn't be in certain  situations but it really shouldn't be  um  and  you know i think the biggest piece of  advice to anyone  re-skilling and  upskilling or thinking about re-entering  the workplace is do as much work on  yourself and understanding yourself and  your drivers as you do on the new skills  that you're learning so that you  confidently present yourself  uh you know in any interview situation  and it's as much about being  balanced and structured in your own  self having that value and net worth as  it is the new skills but i don't think  age has anything to do with it um  there's a great book out there called  the hundred year life and if you haven't  read it i suggest you read it um and it  takes people through the fact that  people are living longer and therefore  they need to consider  their life over a much longer period of  time and that we'll all be thinking  about second and third careers at  different points and learning new skills  uh and getting back in the workplaces  and you know or changing tap completely  and going and doing something totally  different and it's a really uplifting uh  read so  check it out um not that i'm here to  plug it it's nothing to do with me but  uh you know i personally don't think it  should make any difference  that's very encouraging  so let me um  close out the  session by saying thank you to our  panelists  um  krishna and russell both have linkedin  profiles i have  i'm putting the  linkedin  profile links into the chat so that you  can access directly there  uh  with that i'd just like to  ask people to stay in touch with us  on our social channels at simplylearn  on facebook and twitter  and on linkedin we're easy to find after  simply learn  russell do you have such channels that  you'd like to promote  yeah if you're if you if you if you just  google  ilex group we've got facebook  twitter channels linkedin channels like  the all the usual socials you'll find us  there you might even find us on  instagram these days  okay and  again thank you both for being here and  for a very lively discussion  we will be sending a link to the  discussion recording to our attendees  and we'll also be sending a link to the  white paper that we referenced in the  presentation  so uh look for that in your email in the  next few days  and to our  attendees from india uh  join us diwali holiday and  thank you all for being here that  includes our webinar today  thank you  and have a good one bye bye thank you  very much everybody thank you  oh  you
 

